[Workshop05Demo/README.md] # Threading — Workshop 05 Demo

Companion code for `Week06_Basics.pptx` and `Week06_Questions_Bank.pptx` (.NET Track).

Target framework: **net8.0** · `ImplicitUsings` **disabled** (every `using` is written out) · `Nullable` **enabled**.

## Structure

```
Workshop05Demo.sln
├── ThreadingLib/                            class library
│   ├── Basics/ThreadBasics.cs                   Q1 – Q3
│   ├── Background/BackgroundTasks.cs            Q4  foreground vs background
│   ├── Background/PoolTasks.cs                  Q5  ThreadPool + CountdownEvent
│   ├── Sync/RaceCounter.cs                      Q6 – Q7  race, lock, Interlocked
│   └── Sync/DeadlockLab.cs                      Q8  deadlock and the fix
└── Workshop05.ConsoleApp/
    ├── Program.cs                           menu + the two Q4 command-line runs
    ├── AnswerKey.cs                         runs all 8 questions
    └── Demos/
        ├── ThreadBasicsDemo.cs
        ├── BackgroundAndPoolDemo.cs
        └── SynchronizationDemo.cs
```

## Run it

```bash
dotnet run --project Workshop05.ConsoleApp
```

Or open `Workshop05Demo.sln` in Visual Studio, set **Workshop05.ConsoleApp** as the startup project, and press F5.

## Menu

| Option | What it shows | Slide |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | the four ways to start a thread, the loop-capture bug side by side with its fix, `IsAlive` / `ThreadState` through a thread's life, and `Join` making the last line deterministic | Creating a Thread · The Thread Object · Join and Sleep |
| 2 | `IsBackground` before vs after `Start()`, pool threads being background, `CountdownEvent`, and a timed 200-threads vs 200-pool-items comparison | Foreground vs Background · Thread Pool |
| 3 | the same counter run three times unprotected (three different wrong answers), then `lock` and `Interlocked` timed against each other, then a deadlock and its fix | Race Condition · lock · lock vs Interlocked · Deadlock |
| 4 | every question's expected output | Questions Bank |

## Q4 needs its own run

Foreground vs background only shows itself when `Main` actually **returns**. Inside a menu-driven app the process never ends, so a background thread always gets to finish — which hides the whole point. Two extra entry points exist for exactly this:

```bash
dotnet run --project Workshop05.ConsoleApp -- q4-foreground   # prints Worker 1, 2, 3
dotnet run --project Workshop05.ConsoleApp -- q4-background   # prints nothing after "Main ends here"
```

Run them back to back in the terminal. The second one is the moment the lesson lands.

## Notes for the session

* **Q6 and Q7 print a different number on every run.** That is the answer, not a bug — the answer key deliberately runs each one twice so the two results disagree on screen.
* **The deadlock version really would hang** with a plain nested `lock`. `Monitor.TryEnter` with a 1000 ms timeout is what turns the freeze into a printed message, so the program can *report* the deadlock instead of becoming it.
* **Timings are included on purpose** in options 2 and 3 — the pool-vs-new-thread cost and the `lock`-vs-`Interlocked` cost are far more convincing as numbers than as claims. They vary by machine.
* `ThreadBasicsDemo` shows the loop-capture bug and its one-line fix next to each other; the buggy version usually prints `3 3 3`, but on a fast machine it can occasionally print something else — that variability is itself worth pointing out.
* No `Task`, `async`/`await` or `Parallel.For` is used anywhere. The last slide points at them; this code stays on raw threads.

[Workshop05Demo/ThreadingLib/Background/BackgroundTasks.cs] using System;
using System.Threading;

namespace ThreadingLib.Background
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Model answer for Question 4.
    ///
    /// The real difference only shows when Main RETURNS, so the console app
    /// exposes these through command-line switches:
    ///
    ///   dotnet run --project Workshop05.ConsoleApp -- q4-foreground
    ///   dotnet run --project Workshop05.ConsoleApp -- q4-background
    ///
    /// Inside the menu the process never ends, so the background worker
    /// would still get to finish - which is exactly why it needs its own run.
    /// </summary>
    public static class BackgroundTasks
    {
        private static void Work()
        {
            for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++)
            {
                Thread.Sleep(300);
                Console.WriteLine($"Worker {i}");
            }
        }

        /// <summary>Foreground (the default): the process waits for it.</summary>
        public static void RunForeground()
        {
            Thread worker = new Thread(Work);

            // worker.IsBackground is false by default - nothing to set
            worker.Start();

            Console.WriteLine("Main ends here");

            // Main returns now. The app stays alive until Work() finishes,
            // so all three Worker lines still appear.
        }

        /// <summary>Background: the process does NOT wait for it.</summary>
        public static void RunBackground()
        {
            Thread worker = new Thread(Work);

            worker.IsBackground = true;   // MUST be set before Start()
            worker.Start();

            Console.WriteLine("Main ends here");

            // Main returns now. The worker is killed mid-sleep:
            // no exception, no finally block, no message.
        }
    }
}

[Workshop05Demo/ThreadingLib/Background/PoolTasks.cs] using System;
using System.Threading;

namespace ThreadingLib.Background
{
    /// <summary>Model answer for Question 5.</summary>
    public static class PoolTasks
    {
        /// <summary>
        /// Queues count work items on the pool. Item i writes i * i into its own
        /// slot, so - exactly like Q3 - no lock is needed anywhere.
        /// </summary>
        public static int[] SquaresOnPool(int count)
        {
            int[] results = new int[count];

            using (CountdownEvent done = new CountdownEvent(count))
            {
                for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
                {
                    int index = i;               // copy the loop variable!

                    ThreadPool.QueueUserWorkItem(state =>
                    {
                        results[index] = index * index;
                        done.Signal();           // one down
                    });
                }

                done.Wait();                     // block until every Signal() has landed
            }

            return results;
        }

        /// <summary>Every thread pool thread is a background thread. Always.</summary>
        public static bool PoolThreadsAreBackground()
        {
            bool isBackground = false;

            using (CountdownEvent done = new CountdownEvent(1))
            {
                ThreadPool.QueueUserWorkItem(state =>
                {
                    isBackground = Thread.CurrentThread.IsBackground;
                    done.Signal();
                });

                done.Wait();
            }

            return isBackground;
        }
    }
}

